window.ByteCastVoiceoverSections = {"schema": "bytecast-voiceover-sections-v1", "slug": "from_draft_to_deliverable", "code": "AGENT-D9", "narrator": "Byte", "sections": [{"id": "cold_open", "title": "Cold Open", "displayText": "Day 1 taught you to find the door. Day 2 taught you to find the source. Day 3 taught you to turn signal into action. Day 4 taught you to leave the trail. Day 5 taught you to work the queue. Day 6 taught you to protect the boundary. Day 7 taught you to serve without exposing the system. Day 8 taught you to write clearly, usefully, and with evidence.\n\nDay 9 teaches you how to finish.\n\nNot imagine. Not expand. Not improve forever. Not open another front because the current one got boring.\n\nFinish.\n\nAeroVista has no shortage of drafts: scripts, pages, docs, offers, flyers, dashboards, reports, systems, ideas, training paths, client concepts, release plans.\n\nBut a draft does not create value until it becomes a deliverable.\n\nToday, we learn the discipline of finishing the slice.\n\nWelcome to Day 9.\n\nFrom Draft to Deliverable.", "speakText": "Day 1 taught you to find the door. Day 2 taught you to find the source. Day 3 taught you to turn signal into action. Day 4 taught you to leave the trail. Day 5 taught you to work the queue. Day 6 taught you to protect the boundary. Day 7 taught you to serve without exposing the system. Day 8 taught you to write clearly, usefully, and with evidence. Day 9 teaches you how to finish. Not imagine. Not expand. Not improve forever. Not open another front because the current one got boring. Finish. AeroVista has no shortage of drafts: scripts, pages, docs, offers, flyers, dashboards, reports, systems, ideas, training paths, client concepts, release plans. But a draft does not create value until it becomes a deliverable. Today, we learn the discipline of finishing the slice. Welcome to Day 9. From Draft to Deliverable."}, {"id": "a_draft_is_not_a_deliverable", "title": "A Draft Is Not a Deliverable", "displayText": "A draft is a working shape.\n\nA deliverable is a usable output.\n\nDo not confuse them.\n\nA draft can be messy. A draft can be private. A draft can contain notes. A draft can be incomplete. A draft can ask questions. A draft can explore options.\n\nA deliverable is different.\n\nA deliverable has a purpose, audience, format, acceptance criteria, and boundaries. A deliverable is safe for the intended reader. A deliverable can be handed off, published, reviewed, used, or approved.\n\nInside AeroVista, agents must know which state they are in.\n\nIf it is drafted, say drafted. If it is ready for review, say ready for review. If it is approved, say approved. If it is published, say published. If it is live, say live. If it is adopted, say adopted.\n\nA draft pretending to be a deliverable creates fog.\n\nA deliverable mislabeled as a draft creates wasted rework.\n\nUse the right state.", "speakText": "A draft is a working shape. A deliverable is a usable output. Do not confuse them. A draft can be messy. A draft can be private. A draft can contain notes. A draft can be incomplete. A draft can ask questions. A draft can explore options. A deliverable is different. A deliverable has a purpose, audience, format, acceptance criteria, and boundaries. A deliverable is safe for the intended reader. A deliverable can be handed off, published, reviewed, used, or approved. Inside AeroVista, agents must know which state they are in. If it is drafted, say drafted. If it is ready for review, say ready for review. If it is approved, say approved. If it is published, say published. If it is live, say live. If it is adopted, say adopted. A draft pretending to be a deliverable creates fog. A deliverable mislabeled as a draft creates wasted rework. Use the right state."}, {"id": "the_slice_principle", "title": "The Slice Principle", "displayText": "AeroVista is wide. That is why agents must finish slices.\n\nA slice is a small complete unit of value.\n\nNot the entire platform. Not the entire system. Not all future versions. Not every possible feature. Not a perfect cathedral.\n\nA slice.\n\nExamples:\n\nOne Day 9 script plus one completion drill.\n\nOne public booth page plus one verified public link.\n\nOne client one-pager plus one next-step email.\n\nOne EOD Capsule template plus one example entry.\n\nOne release status update plus one evidence note.\n\nOne access fix plus one verification pass.\n\nOne ByteCast episode page plus one audio player placeholder and one completion card.\n\nA slice should be small enough to finish, but complete enough to use.\n\nThat is the sweet spot.\n\nIf a task keeps expanding, ask:\n\nWhat is the smallest useful version? Who needs it? What does done mean? What can wait? What must be true before delivery? What proof shows it works?\n\nFinish that.\n\nThen decide the next slice.", "speakText": "AeroVista is wide. That is why agents must finish slices. A slice is a small complete unit of value. Not the entire platform. Not the entire system. Not all future versions. Not every possible feature. Not a perfect cathedral. A slice. Examples: One Day 9 script plus one completion drill. One public booth page plus one verified public link. One client one-pager plus one next-step email. One EOD Capsule template plus one example entry. One release status update plus one evidence note. One access fix plus one verification pass. One ByteCast episode page plus one audio player placeholder and one completion card. A slice should be small enough to finish, but complete enough to use. That is the sweet spot. If a task keeps expanding, ask: What is the smallest useful version? Who needs it? What does done mean? What can wait? What must be true before delivery? What proof shows it works? Finish that. Then decide the next slice."}, {"id": "define_done_before_you_polish", "title": "Define Done Before You Polish", "displayText": "Polishing before defining done is how work becomes endless.\n\nBefore you polish, define done.\n\nUse the Done Definition.\n\nOne: Purpose\n\nWhy does this deliverable exist?\n\nTrain agents. Inform a client. Publish a page. Resolve access. Update a release. Capture a decision. Get approval. Reduce risk. Move a prospect forward.\n\nTwo: Audience\n\nWho will use it?\n\nFounder. Operator. Builder. Client. Prospect. Public visitor. Agent. Technical maintainer. Legal reviewer. Shareholder.\n\nThree: Output\n\nWhat exactly will exist?\n\nScript. Drill. HTML page. ZIP pack. Handoff. Email. Proposal. Release note. Registry update. Audio file. Checklist. Dashboard card. Smoke result.\n\nFour: Acceptance Criteria\n\nWhat must be true for this to count?\n\nExamples:\n\n“The script includes all required segments and completion drill.”\n\n“The public page loads at the intended URL.”\n\n“The client update is safe to send and includes one next action.”\n\n“The release note names state, evidence, risk, and owner.”\n\n“The ZIP includes all expected files and a README.”\n\n“The access fix is verified for founder, operator, builder, and public roles.”\n\nAcceptance criteria are the finish line. No acceptance criteria, no finish line.\n\nFive: Out of Scope\n\nWhat are we not doing in this slice?\n\nExample:\n\n“Out of scope: audio production, HTML conversion, completion tracking, and visual design.”\n\nThat sentence protects the work.\n\nWithout out-of-scope boundaries, every draft becomes a galaxy.", "speakText": "Polishing before defining done is how work becomes endless. Before you polish, define done. Use the Done Definition. One: Purpose Why does this deliverable exist? Train agents. Inform a client. Publish a page. Resolve access. Update a release. Capture a decision. Get approval. Reduce risk. Move a prospect forward. Two: Audience Who will use it? Founder. Operator. Builder. Client. Prospect. Public visitor. Agent. Technical maintainer. Legal reviewer. Shareholder. Three: Output What exactly will exist? Script. Drill. HTML page. ZIP pack. Handoff. Email. Proposal. Release note. Registry update. Audio file. Checklist. Dashboard card. Smoke result. Four: Acceptance Criteria What must be true for this to count? Examples: “The script includes all required segments and completion drill.” “The public page loads at the intended URL.” “The client update is safe to send and includes one next action.” “The release note names state, evidence, risk, and owner.” “The ZIP includes all expected files and a README.” “The access fix is verified for founder, operator, builder, and public roles.” Acceptance criteria are the finish line. No acceptance criteria, no finish line. Five: Out of Scope What are we not doing in this slice? Example: “Out of scope: audio production, HTML conversion, completion tracking, and visual design.” That sentence protects the work. Without out-of-scope boundaries, every draft becomes a galaxy."}, {"id": "the_polish_ladder", "title": "The Polish Ladder", "displayText": "Not all polish matters equally.\n\nUse the Polish Ladder.\n\nLevel One: Correct\n\nIs it true? Names, dates, states, links, roles, claims, citations, boundaries, and instructions must be correct. If it is not correct, do not polish the vibe. Fix the truth.\n\nLevel Two: Complete\n\nDoes it include the required parts? For a script: title, audience, runtime, segments, closing, completion drill. For a handoff: touched, changed, verified, unknowns, risks, next action. Complete means the deliverable can do its job.\n\nLevel Three: Clear\n\nCan the intended audience understand it quickly? Cut bloat. Define terms. Use simple state labels. Lead with the point. End with the next action.\n\nLevel Four: Safe\n\nIs it safe for the intended audience? No secrets. No private logs. No other builder data. No internal machinery in public. No unapproved legal terms. No overpromising.\n\nLevel Five: Useful\n\nCan the reader act from it? If not, add the missing action, owner, or decision.\n\nLevel Six: On-Brand\n\nDoes it feel like AeroVista? Clear. Gritty but intelligent. Practical. Slightly cinematic when useful. No empty sci-fi fog.\n\nLevel Seven: Beautiful\n\nOnly after the first six.\n\nMake it elegant. Make it memorable. Make it sing.\n\nBut never make it beautiful before it is correct, complete, clear, safe, and useful.\n\nA pretty broken deliverable is still broken.", "speakText": "Not all polish matters equally. Use the Polish Ladder. Level One: Correct Is it true? Names, dates, states, links, roles, claims, citations, boundaries, and instructions must be correct. If it is not correct, do not polish the vibe. Fix the truth. Level Two: Complete Does it include the required parts? For a script: title, audience, runtime, segments, closing, completion drill. For a handoff: touched, changed, verified, unknowns, risks, next action. Complete means the deliverable can do its job. Level Three: Clear Can the intended audience understand it quickly? Cut bloat. Define terms. Use simple state labels. Lead with the point. End with the next action. Level Four: Safe Is it safe for the intended audience? No secrets. No private logs. No other builder data. No internal machinery in public. No unapproved legal terms. No overpromising. Level Five: Useful Can the reader act from it? If not, add the missing action, owner, or decision. Level Six: On-Brand Does it feel like AeroVista? Clear. Gritty but intelligent. Practical. Slightly cinematic when useful. No empty sci-fi fog. Level Seven: Beautiful Only after the first six. Make it elegant. Make it memorable. Make it sing. But never make it beautiful before it is correct, complete, clear, safe, and useful. A pretty broken deliverable is still broken."}, {"id": "scope_creep_the_silent_draft_killer", "title": "Scope Creep: The Silent Draft Killer", "displayText": "Scope creep is how drafts die.\n\nIt starts innocently.\n\n“While we are here, let’s add audio.”\n\n“Maybe this should be a full course.”\n\n“What if we also make a dashboard?”\n\n“Could we turn this into a public landing page?”\n\n“Should we create a workflow automation too?”\n\n“Let’s also build the whole training hub.”\n\nMaybe yes. But not inside this slice.\n\nAeroVista agents must learn the Scope Stop.\n\nWhen a new idea appears, ask:\n\nDoes this belong to the current deliverable? Is it required by acceptance criteria? Does it reduce current risk? Does it block delivery? Does the owner approve expanding scope? Can it become the next slice instead?\n\nMost good ideas should be captured, not crammed.\n\nUse this sentence:\n\n“That is a good next-slice candidate, but it is out of scope for this delivery.”\n\nThat sentence saves projects.\n\nIt keeps momentum honest.", "speakText": "Scope creep is how drafts die. It starts innocently. “While we are here, let’s add audio.” “Maybe this should be a full course.” “What if we also make a dashboard?” “Could we turn this into a public landing page?” “Should we create a workflow automation too?” “Let’s also build the whole training hub.” Maybe yes. But not inside this slice. AeroVista agents must learn the Scope Stop. When a new idea appears, ask: Does this belong to the current deliverable? Is it required by acceptance criteria? Does it reduce current risk? Does it block delivery? Does the owner approve expanding scope? Can it become the next slice instead? Most good ideas should be captured, not crammed. Use this sentence: “That is a good next-slice candidate, but it is out of scope for this delivery.” That sentence saves projects. It keeps momentum honest."}, {"id": "review_without_reopening_everything", "title": "Review Without Reopening Everything", "displayText": "Review is necessary. Reopening everything is not.\n\nA review pass should match the deliverable state.\n\nDraft Review\n\nDraft review asks: Is the concept right? Is the structure right? Are we missing major pieces? Is the audience correct? Is this worth continuing?\n\nDraft review can be broad.\n\nDelivery Review\n\nDelivery review asks: Does this meet acceptance criteria? Is it accurate? Is it safe? Is it complete? Is the next action clear? Can it be used now?\n\nDelivery review should not reopen the entire concept unless a major risk appears.\n\nFinal Review\n\nFinal review asks: Are there typos? Are links right? Is formatting clean? Is the file named correctly? Is the handoff written? Is the state updated?\n\nFinal review is not the time to reinvent the project.\n\nAgents must protect the review stage.\n\nIf the review starts expanding, say:\n\n“That belongs in a new queue item unless it blocks acceptance.”\n\nThis is how deliverables actually leave the room.", "speakText": "Review is necessary. Reopening everything is not. A review pass should match the deliverable state. Draft Review Draft review asks: Is the concept right? Is the structure right? Are we missing major pieces? Is the audience correct? Is this worth continuing? Draft review can be broad. Delivery Review Delivery review asks: Does this meet acceptance criteria? Is it accurate? Is it safe? Is it complete? Is the next action clear? Can it be used now? Delivery review should not reopen the entire concept unless a major risk appears. Final Review Final review asks: Are there typos? Are links right? Is formatting clean? Is the file named correctly? Is the handoff written? Is the state updated? Final review is not the time to reinvent the project. Agents must protect the review stage. If the review starts expanding, say: “That belongs in a new queue item unless it blocks acceptance.” This is how deliverables actually leave the room."}, {"id": "acceptance_criteria_and_evidence", "title": "Acceptance Criteria and Evidence", "displayText": "A deliverable should not be accepted on vibes.\n\nUse evidence.\n\nFor a document: does it include required sections, audience, truth labels, sensitive-content removal, next action, and clean file placement?\n\nFor a ZIP pack: does it include all files, README, manifest, master reference, and a clear filename? Does the archive open?\n\nFor a public page: does it load, work on the intended device, respect public/private boundaries, include correct links, and present a clear call to action?\n\nFor an access change: was it tested by role, scoped narrowly, audited, and documented safely?\n\nFor a client deliverable: does it match scope, avoid unapproved promises, identify client next steps, and respect IP/confidentiality terms?\n\nEvidence turns “looks good” into “ready.”\n\nAeroVista does not need fake certainty.\n\nIt needs clean acceptance.", "speakText": "A deliverable should not be accepted on vibes. Use evidence. For a document: does it include required sections, audience, truth labels, sensitive-content removal, next action, and clean file placement? For a ZIP pack: does it include all files, README, manifest, master reference, and a clear filename? Does the archive open? For a public page: does it load, work on the intended device, respect public/private boundaries, include correct links, and present a clear call to action? For an access change: was it tested by role, scoped narrowly, audited, and documented safely? For a client deliverable: does it match scope, avoid unapproved promises, identify client next steps, and respect IP/confidentiality terms? Evidence turns “looks good” into “ready.” AeroVista does not need fake certainty. It needs clean acceptance."}, {"id": "the_delivery_state_ladder", "title": "The Delivery State Ladder", "displayText": "Use the Delivery State Ladder.\n\nDrafted: the first usable shape exists. Not final. Not approved. Not published.\n\nReady for Review: the draft is complete enough for someone to evaluate against the goal.\n\nRevision Needed: review found required changes. Not failure. Just state.\n\nReady for Delivery: acceptance criteria are met, and only final packaging remains.\n\nDelivered: the output has been sent, published, packaged, deployed, or handed off to the intended lane.\n\nVerified: the delivered output was checked in the target environment.\n\nAdopted: people are actually using it.\n\nDo not skip rungs.\n\nDo not call drafted work delivered. Do not call delivered work verified. Do not call verified work adopted.\n\nPrecision protects the system.", "speakText": "Use the Delivery State Ladder. Drafted: the first usable shape exists. Not final. Not approved. Not published. Ready for Review: the draft is complete enough for someone to evaluate against the goal. Revision Needed: review found required changes. Not failure. Just state. Ready for Delivery: acceptance criteria are met, and only final packaging remains. Delivered: the output has been sent, published, packaged, deployed, or handed off to the intended lane. Verified: the delivered output was checked in the target environment. Adopted: people are actually using it. Do not skip rungs. Do not call drafted work delivered. Do not call delivered work verified. Do not call verified work adopted. Precision protects the system."}, {"id": "the_finish_line_checklist", "title": "The Finish Line Checklist", "displayText": "Before calling a slice finished, run the Finish Line Checklist.\n\nPurpose clear? Audience clear? Output exists? Acceptance criteria met? Out-of-scope items captured separately? Evidence attached? Sensitive material removed or restricted? State label correct? Owner identified? Next action named? Handoff written? Queue updated? Memory or documentation update needed? Release or registry update needed? File name and location clean? Reviewer or approver identified if needed?\n\nIf the answer is no, finish the missing piece.\n\nIf the missing piece is outside the slice, capture it as a next-slice item.\n\nDo not let it block the current deliverable unless it affects acceptance.\n\nThat is how agents finish without becoming reckless.", "speakText": "Before calling a slice finished, run the Finish Line Checklist. Purpose clear? Audience clear? Output exists? Acceptance criteria met? Out-of-scope items captured separately? Evidence attached? Sensitive material removed or restricted? State label correct? Owner identified? Next action named? Handoff written? Queue updated? Memory or documentation update needed? Release or registry update needed? File name and location clean? Reviewer or approver identified if needed? If the answer is no, finish the missing piece. If the missing piece is outside the slice, capture it as a next-slice item. Do not let it block the current deliverable unless it affects acceptance. That is how agents finish without becoming reckless."}, {"id": "finishing_with_discipline", "title": "Finishing With Discipline", "displayText": "There is a moment in every task where the agent has to stop expanding and start finishing.\n\nThat moment matters.\n\nIf you keep improving forever, you do not deliver.\n\nIf you deliver too early, you create rework.\n\nThe discipline is knowing the difference.\n\nAsk:\n\nWould another round materially improve usefulness? Or am I avoiding the handoff? Would more polish reduce risk? Or am I chasing taste? Is this missing an acceptance requirement? Or am I adding a future feature? Does the user need this now? Or do they need the larger system later?\n\nAeroVista’s best work is not unfinished perfection.\n\nIt is finished usefulness that can evolve.\n\nShip the slice.\n\nLeave the trail.\n\nCapture the next slice.\n\nMove.", "speakText": "There is a moment in every task where the agent has to stop expanding and start finishing. That moment matters. If you keep improving forever, you do not deliver. If you deliver too early, you create rework. The discipline is knowing the difference. Ask: Would another round materially improve usefulness? Or am I avoiding the handoff? Would more polish reduce risk? Or am I chasing taste? Is this missing an acceptance requirement? Or am I adding a future feature? Does the user need this now? Or do they need the larger system later? AeroVista’s best work is not unfinished perfection. It is finished usefulness that can evolve. Ship the slice. Leave the trail. Capture the next slice. Move."}, {"id": "confidentiality_at_delivery", "title": "Confidentiality at Delivery", "displayText": "Delivery is a risk point.\n\nBefore delivery, check boundaries.\n\nDoes this include client information, builder data, private emails, internal URLs, access details, source paths, unreleased strategy, legal language, pricing exceptions, IP terms, credentials, logs, or screenshots with sensitive details?\n\nIf yes, slow down.\n\nEither remove, summarize, restrict, or escalate.\n\nDo not ship sensitive material because you are excited to finish.\n\nFinishing fast is good.\n\nFinishing safely is mandatory.", "speakText": "Delivery is a risk point. Before delivery, check boundaries. Does this include client information, builder data, private emails, internal URLs, access details, source paths, unreleased strategy, legal language, pricing exceptions, IP terms, credentials, logs, or screenshots with sensitive details? If yes, slow down. Either remove, summarize, restrict, or escalate. Do not ship sensitive material because you are excited to finish. Finishing fast is good. Finishing safely is mandatory."}, {"id": "closing", "title": "Closing", "displayText": "You now have nine laws.\n\nFind the door. Find the source. Turn signal into action. Leave the trail. Work the queue. Protect the boundary. Serve without exposing the system. Write clearly, usefully, and with evidence. Finish the slice.\n\nAeroVista does not need infinite drafts.\n\nIt needs useful deliverables.\n\nDefine done. Protect scope. Polish in the right order. Verify acceptance. Label the state. Package the output. Leave the handoff. Capture the next slice.\n\nThat is how work becomes real.\n\nFrom draft to deliverable.\n\nThat is how vision takes flight.", "speakText": "You now have nine laws. Find the door. Find the source. Turn signal into action. Leave the trail. Work the queue. Protect the boundary. Serve without exposing the system. Write clearly, usefully, and with evidence. Finish the slice. AeroVista does not need infinite drafts. It needs useful deliverables. Define done. Protect scope. Polish in the right order. Verify acceptance. Label the state. Package the output. Leave the handoff. Capture the next slice. That is how work becomes real. From draft to deliverable. That is how vision takes flight."}], "overviewOrder": ["cold_open", "a_draft_is_not_a_deliverable", "the_slice_principle", "define_done_before_you_polish", "the_polish_ladder", "scope_creep_the_silent_draft_killer", "review_without_reopening_everything", "acceptance_criteria_and_evidence", "the_delivery_state_ladder", "the_finish_line_checklist", "finishing_with_discipline", "confidentiality_at_delivery", "closing"], "heroSectionId": "cold_open", "segmentOrder": ["cold_open", "a_draft_is_not_a_deliverable", "the_slice_principle", "define_done_before_you_polish", "the_polish_ladder", "scope_creep_the_silent_draft_killer", "review_without_reopening_everything", "acceptance_criteria_and_evidence", "the_delivery_state_ladder", "the_finish_line_checklist", "finishing_with_discipline", "confidentiality_at_delivery", "closing"]};
